1. What Is Abstraction?

Abstraction means exposing what an object can do while hiding unnecessary implementation details.

Consider a car.

You know:

Start()
Accelerate()
Brake()
Turn()

But you don't need to know every internal engine operation to drive it.

Conceptually:

        User
          ↓
    Car controls
          ↓
 ┌──────────────────┐
 │ Start()          │
 │ Accelerate()     │
 │ Brake()          │
 └──────────────────┘
          ↓
   Hidden internals

The user interacts with a simple interface while the implementation remains hidden.

2. Abstraction vs Encapsulation

These concepts are related but different.

Encapsulation

Encapsulation focuses on:

Protecting and controlling access to data and implementation.

Example:

class BankAccount
{
    private decimal balance;

    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount > 0)
        {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }
}

balance is hidden.

Abstraction

Abstraction focuses on:

Defining what something should do while hiding unnecessary implementation details.

For example:

abstract class Payment
{
    public abstract void Pay(decimal amount);
}

We are saying:

Every payment type must provide a Pay() operation.

But we don't define the complete payment mechanism here.

Simple Difference
Encapsulation
    ↓
How do I protect/control the internals?

Abstraction
    ↓
What should this object expose?
3. Why Do We Need Abstraction?

Suppose we have:

Credit Card Payment
UPI Payment
PayPal Payment
Bank Transfer

Every payment method needs something like:

Pay(amount)

But the implementation is different.

Credit Card
    ↓
Card processing

UPI
    ↓
UPI processing

Bank Transfer
    ↓
Bank processing

We want a common concept:

             Payment
                ↓
        Pay(amount)
                ↑
        ┌───────┼────────┐
        │       │        │
      Card     UPI     Bank

This is where abstraction becomes useful.

4. Abstract Classes

C# provides the abstract keyword.

Example:

abstract class Payment
{
    public abstract void Pay(decimal amount);
}

This is an abstract class.

It represents a general concept rather than a directly usable concrete object.

5. Abstract Class Cannot Be Instantiated

This is important.

You cannot do:

Payment payment = new Payment();

This is invalid because Payment is abstract.

Instead, create a concrete derived class:

class CreditCardPayment : Payment
{
    public override void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Paid {amount} using credit card.");
    }
}

Now:

Payment payment = new CreditCardPayment();

payment.Pay(500);

This is valid.

Mental model:

abstract class
      ↓
defines a general concept
      ↓
derived class
      ↓
provides concrete implementation
6. Abstract Method

An abstract method has no implementation in the abstract base class.

Example:

abstract class Payment
{
    public abstract void Pay(decimal amount);
}

Notice:

public abstract void Pay(decimal amount);

There is no:

{ }

body.

The method declaration says:

A derived concrete class must provide the implementation.

7. Implementing an Abstract Method

Derived class:

class CreditCardPayment : Payment
{
    public override void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(
            $"Paid {amount} using credit card."
        );
    }
}

The important keyword is:

override

The abstract method requires the derived class to provide an implementation.

So:

Payment
   ↓
abstract Pay()
   ↓
CreditCardPayment
   ↓
override Pay()
8. Another Example
abstract class Animal
{
    public abstract void MakeSound();
}

Now:

class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Bark");
    }
}

And:

class Cat : Animal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Meow");
    }
}

Usage:

Animal dog = new Dog();
Animal cat = new Cat();

dog.MakeSound();
cat.MakeSound();

Output:

Bark
Meow

Notice something important:

Animal
   ↓
defines WHAT
   ↓
MakeSound()

Dog
   ↓
defines HOW
   ↓
Bark

Cat
   ↓
defines HOW
   ↓
Meow

That is abstraction.

9. Abstract Class Can Have Normal Methods

An abstract class is not limited to abstract methods.

It can contain:

fields
properties
constructors
normal methods
abstract methods
static members
protected members

Example:

abstract class Animal
{
    protected string name;

    public Animal(string name)
    {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void Sleep()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{name} is sleeping.");
    }

    public abstract void MakeSound();
}

Derived class:

class Dog : Animal
{
    public Dog(string name)
        : base(name)
    {
    }

    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Bark");
    }
}

Usage:

Dog dog = new Dog("Tommy");

dog.Sleep();
dog.MakeSound();

Output:

Tommy is sleeping.
Bark
10. Why Mix Abstract and Concrete Members?

Because the base class can provide common functionality while requiring derived classes to provide specific functionality.

Example:

Animal
│
├── Sleep()        ← common implementation
│
└── MakeSound()    ← different for every animal

So:

Common behavior
    ↓
Normal method

Variable behavior
    ↓
Abstract method

This is one of the strongest use cases for abstract classes.

11. Abstract Class With Constructor

An abstract class can have a constructor.

Example:

abstract class Animal
{
    protected string name;

    protected Animal(string name)
    {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public abstract void MakeSound();
}

Then:

class Dog : Animal
{
    public Dog(string name)
        : base(name)
    {
    }

    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Bark");
    }
}

Even though you cannot create:

new Animal(...)

the Animal constructor still runs when creating a derived object.

new Dog("Tommy")
      ↓
Animal constructor
      ↓
Dog constructor
      ↓
Dog object

This connects directly to the inheritance and constructor chaining lesson.

12. Abstract Class Does Not Mean "Everything Is Abstract"

This is a common misconception.

This is completely valid:

abstract class Vehicle
{
    public void Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Starting");
    }

    public abstract void Move();
}

Here:

Vehicle
├── Start()
│   └── concrete implementation
│
└── Move()
    └── abstract requirement

So an abstract class can contain both:

implemented behavior
+
required behavior
13. What If a Derived Class Doesn't Implement the Abstract Method?

Suppose:

abstract class Animal
{
    public abstract void MakeSound();
}

Then:

class Dog : Animal
{
}

This is invalid if Dog is concrete.

Why?

Because Dog still has an unimplemented abstract member.

It must provide:

public override void MakeSound()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Bark");
}

Alternatively, Dog itself could also be abstract:

abstract class Dog : Animal
{
}

Then another concrete class can implement the method later.

14. Abstract Class Hierarchy

You can have:

Animal
  ↓
Mammal
  ↓
Dog

where intermediate classes can also remain abstract.

Example:

abstract class Animal
{
    public abstract void MakeSound();
}

abstract class Mammal : Animal
{
    public void Breathe()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Breathing");
    }
}

class Dog : Mammal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Bark");
    }
}

Mammal doesn't have to implement MakeSound() because it is also abstract.

15. Now: What Is an Interface?

An interface defines a contract that a type can implement.

Example:

interface IPayment
{
    void Pay(decimal amount);
}

This says:

Any class implementing IPayment must provide Pay(decimal).

Example:

class CreditCardPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(
            $"Paid {amount} using credit card."
        );
    }
}

Usage:

IPayment payment = new CreditCardPayment();

payment.Pay(500);
16. Interface Mental Model

Think of an interface as a contract.

IPayment
   │
   └── Pay(amount)

Any implementing class agrees:

"I provide this operation."

Different classes can implement it differently:

             IPayment
            /        \
           /          \
    CreditCard        UPI
       │                │
   Pay()              Pay()

The contract is common.

The implementation is different.

17. Interface Syntax

Modern C#:

interface IPayment
{
    void Pay(decimal amount);
}

Implementation:

class CreditCardPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Credit card payment");
    }
}

The syntax looks similar to inheritance:

class Dog : Animal

and:

class Dog : IAnimal

But conceptually they are different.

Base class
    ↓
inheritance

Interface
    ↓
contract implementation
18. Why Does an Interface Name Usually Start With I?

C# convention uses:

I + descriptive name

Examples:

IEnumerable
IDisposable
IComparable
IPayment
ILogger
IRepository

So:

interface IPayment

is conventional.

This is a naming convention, not a language requirement.

19. Abstract Class vs Interface

This is one of the most important comparisons in C#.

Abstract Class	Interface
Represents a base type/concept	Represents a contract/capability
Uses abstract class	Uses interface
Can have fields	Cannot have instance fields in the same way a class does
Can have constructors	Cannot have constructors
Can have implemented methods	Can define contract members and, in modern C#, default implementations
Can have abstract methods	Can declare interface members
A class can inherit only one class	A class can implement multiple interfaces
Can have access modifiers on members	Interface members have interface-specific accessibility rules
Supports class inheritance	Supports contract implementation

The biggest practical difference:

Class inheritance
    ↓
One base class

Interface implementation
    ↓
Multiple interfaces

Example:

class SmartPhone : Device, ICamera, IPhone, IGps
{
}

A class can inherit from one base class while implementing several interfaces.

20. Abstract Class Is About "What You Are"

An abstract class often represents a fundamental type relationship.

Example:

Dog IS-A Animal
Car IS-A Vehicle
Manager IS-A Employee

So:

abstract class Animal
{
}
21. Interface Is Often About "What You Can Do"

An interface can represent a capability.

For example:

interface IFlyable
{
    void Fly();
}

Then:

class Bird : IFlyable
{
    public void Fly()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Bird flying");
    }
}

Another completely different class can implement it:

class Airplane : IFlyable
{
    public void Fly()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Airplane flying");
    }
}

They don't need to share a base class.

IFlyable
   ↑
   ├── Bird
   │
   └── Airplane

The common concept is:

They can fly.

22. One Class Can Implement Multiple Interfaces

This is a major advantage.

interface IFlyable
{
    void Fly();
}

interface ISwimmable
{
    void Swim();
}

A class can implement both:

class Duck : IFlyable, ISwimmable
{
    public void Fly()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Duck flying");
    }

    public void Swim()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Duck swimming");
    }
}

Now:

Duck
├── IFlyable
│   └── Fly()
│
└── ISwimmable
    └── Swim()
23. Multiple Interfaces vs Multiple Inheritance

C# does not allow:

class C : A, B

where A and B are classes.

But it allows:

class C : A, IB, IC

where:

A  → class
IB → interface
IC → interface

This provides multiple contracts without multiple base classes.

24. Implicit Interface Implementation

This is the normal way to implement an interface.

Interface:

interface IPayment
{
    void Pay(decimal amount);
}

Class:

class CreditCardPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Credit card payment");
    }
}

The method:

public void Pay(decimal amount)

implicitly implements:

IPayment.Pay()

That's called implicit interface implementation.

25. Why Is It Called "Implicit"?

Because you don't explicitly mention the interface when defining the method.

You write:

public void Pay(decimal amount)

not:

public void IPayment.Pay(decimal amount)

The compiler recognizes that the class method satisfies the interface contract.

26. Explicit Interface Implementation

Now suppose one class implements two interfaces that contain the same method name.

interface IPrinter
{
    void Start();
}

interface IScanner
{
    void Start();
}

A class could implement both explicitly:

class MultiFunctionMachine : IPrinter, IScanner
{
    void IPrinter.Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Printer starting");
    }

    void IScanner.Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Scanner starting");
    }
}

Now there are two different implementations of:

Start()
27. Why Explicit Implementation Is Useful

Suppose:

IPrinter.Start()
      ↓
Printer-specific behavior

IScanner.Start()
      ↓
Scanner-specific behavior

The class can provide both without ambiguity.

Usage:

IPrinter printer = new MultiFunctionMachine();
IScanner scanner = new MultiFunctionMachine();

printer.Start();
scanner.Start();

Output:

Printer starting
Scanner starting
28. Important: Explicit Members Are Not Directly Accessible Through the Class

Consider:

class MultiFunctionMachine : IPrinter, IScanner
{
    void IPrinter.Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Printer");
    }

    void IScanner.Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Scanner");
    }
}

This will not work:

MultiFunctionMachine machine = new MultiFunctionMachine();

// machine.Start(); // Error

Why?

Because the implementations are explicitly associated with their interfaces.

You need:

IPrinter printer = machine;
printer.Start();

or:

IScanner scanner = machine;
scanner.Start();

This is one of the defining characteristics of explicit implementation.

29. Implicit vs Explicit
Implicit	Explicit
Method is normally public	Method explicitly names the interface
Accessible through the class	Accessed through interface reference
Easy/default approach	Useful when resolving conflicts
One implementation can satisfy interface	Different implementation per interface possible

Example:

Implicit
public void Pay(decimal amount)
{
}
Explicit
void IPayment.Pay(decimal amount)
{
}
30. A Real Example of Explicit Implementation

Suppose:

interface IReadable
{
    void Read();
}

interface IWritable
{
    void Write();
}

Class:

class Document : IReadable, IWritable
{
    public void Read()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Reading document");
    }

    public void Write()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Writing document");
    }
}

No conflict exists, so implicit implementation is simple.

But if both interfaces contain:

void Process();

then explicit implementation allows separate behavior.

31. Interface Reference

Just like base-class references:

IPayment payment = new CreditCardPayment();

The object is:

CreditCardPayment

but the reference is:

IPayment

This is extremely powerful because code can depend on the contract rather than a specific implementation.

Example:

void ProcessPayment(IPayment payment)
{
    payment.Pay(500);
}

Now we can pass:

ProcessPayment(new CreditCardPayment());
ProcessPayment(new UpiPayment());

The method doesn't care which concrete payment class it receives.

32. Why Interfaces Are Powerful

Without an interface:

void ProcessPayment(CreditCardPayment payment)
{
}

The method is tightly coupled to:

CreditCardPayment

With:

void ProcessPayment(IPayment payment)
{
}

the method depends on:

IPayment contract

Now:

CreditCardPayment
UpiPayment
BankTransferPayment

can all be used.

This idea becomes extremely important later when we study:

Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

and software architecture.

33. Abstract Class + Interface Together

A class can inherit from an abstract class and implement interfaces.

abstract class Animal
{
    public abstract void MakeSound();
}

interface IFlyable
{
    void Fly();
}

class Bird : Animal, IFlyable
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Chirp");
    }

    public void Fly()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Flying");
    }
}

This means:

Bird
├── inherits Animal
│   └── MakeSound()
│
└── implements IFlyable
    └── Fly()

This is a very common pattern in real C# applications.

34. A Useful Decision Rule

When deciding between abstract class and interface, ask:

Question 1

Is this a strong base-type relationship?

Example:

Dog IS-A Animal

An abstract class may make sense.

Question 2

Is this a capability or contract that unrelated types can share?

Example:

Bird can fly
Airplane can fly
Drone can fly

An interface may make more sense:

IFlyable
35. Important Modern C# Detail

Interfaces in modern C# are more powerful than the traditional idea:

"An interface can contain only abstract methods."

Modern C# supports features such as default interface implementations and static members in interfaces.

For example, interfaces can provide a default implementation in supported C# versions.

However, the core concept you should remember first is still:

Interface
    ↓
Contract
    ↓
Implementing type provides behavior

We will not mix advanced interface features into the fundamentals unnecessarily.

36. Full Example — Abstract Class + Interface
abstract class Vehicle
{
    protected string brand;

    protected Vehicle(string brand)
    {
        this.brand = brand;
    }

    public void Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{brand} is starting.");
    }

    public abstract void Move();
}

Interface:

interface IElectric
{
    void Charge();
}

Concrete class:

class ElectricCar : Vehicle, IElectric
{
    public ElectricCar(string brand)
        : base(brand)
    {
    }

    public override void Move()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{brand} is moving silently.");
    }

    public void Charge()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{brand} is charging.");
    }
}

Usage:

ElectricCar car = new ElectricCar("Tesla");

car.Start();
car.Move();
car.Charge();

Output:

Tesla is starting.
Tesla is moving silently.
Tesla is charging.

The design:

                 Vehicle
                    ↑
                    │
              ElectricCar
                    │
                    └──── IElectric
37. The Deep Mental Model

Think about the three concepts like this:

Inheritance
    ↓
"What kind of thing is this?"
    ↓
IS-A relationship
Abstract Class
    ↓
"What common base behavior/state should related types share?"
Interface
    ↓
"What capability/contract must this type provide?"

Example:

ElectricCar
│
├── IS-A Vehicle
│      ↓
│   abstract class
│
└── CAN Charge
       ↓
    interface
38. Common Mistakes
Mistake 1 — Trying to instantiate an abstract class
abstract class Animal
{
}

This is invalid:

Animal animal = new Animal();
Mistake 2 — Forgetting override

If the base member is abstract:

public abstract void MakeSound();

the concrete derived class needs:

public override void MakeSound()
{
}
Mistake 3 — Confusing interface with object creation

You cannot normally instantiate an interface:

IPayment payment = new IPayment(); // Error

Instead:

IPayment payment = new CreditCardPayment();
Mistake 4 — Forgetting public for implicit implementation

For a normal interface implementation:

class Payment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
    }
}

The method must satisfy the interface's required accessibility.

Mistake 5 — Assuming interfaces are only for inheritance

Interfaces are better understood as contracts/capabilities, not simply "another kind of parent class."

